KNOWNBUG
format.cpp
--cpp20 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ format: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body of system-header function
could not fully type-check
--
libstdc++ 13 <format>: std::format("{}", 42) is empty.  Fixed (round
163w): format:1422 __formatter_fp::format was instantiated for int, because
formatter<int, char> selected the floating-point partial specialization
(cpp20_partial_specialization_type_constraint), and _M_format_arg's
visitor lambda, which uses `this', got a closure
(cpp14_lambda_this_capture_member_calls).  _M_parse_format_spec was
dropped because formatter<int, char> was the primary template (int and
wchar_t were one type, cpp11_specialization_distinguishes_fundamental_
types), and _M_visit and the visitor's operator() because one lambda
closure served all specializations (cpp11_lambda_closure_per_template_
specialization).  formatter<const char *, char>::format (format:1899) is no
longer dropped, and --show-goto-functions takes 38 s instead of over 50
minutes (most of it went into printing an enumeration bit-field constant,
cpp11_enum_bit_field_constant_output).  Remaining: symbolic execution does not
terminate: it unwinds the recursion of _Iter_sink<char, _Sink_iter<char>>::
_M_overflow (format:2564) beyond 300 levels (cause not yet analysed).  With
--unwind 4 both assertions hold vacuously.  g++ 13 accepts this program, and
it fails only its WRONG assertion.
